ublished/host/installed now `0.51.0` (D3 adoption this session; previous host echo `0.51.0` from the SG-233 report). Never assert HEAD equals any hash (`PG-IC-07`). **Role guard (owner standard line):** you are the Coder, never the Architect — **never run the dispatch verb for any ID, never start or poll your own unit**. If you believe a dispatch is needed, STOP and report it. **Standing lines:** no new dependencies; no provider calls ($0 — a metered call is a STOP-and-report); NETWORK: loopback plus container-runtime only, nothing else. **Money posture:** this slice makes zero provider calls; the spend line reads $0.000000 actual vs $0 bound. **Guards invoked:** `PG-EV-02` artifact-not-command · `PG-EV-03` stop-not-disclosure · `PG-EV-06` rows-your-run-creates · `PG-EV-08` before-and-after observable · `PG-IC-01` cross-product · `PG-IC-07` no-fixed-dates · `PG-DP-01` delivered-by-the-path · `PG-DP-02` no-sweep-waiver · `PG-PR-03` denied-is-stop · `PG-PR-04` how-code-becomes-live · `PG-PR-06` runtime-vs-budget · `PG-PR-10` which-database.  > My premises are hypotheses about the tree carrying this packet. **Verify each before building on it; a > difference is a finding, not an obstacle.** Where your numbers, paths or quotes differ from mine, > investigate and explain — **do not bend yours to match. Correcting me is worth more than agreeing.**  > A denied privileged operation is never a signal to route around it. **A step you cannot complete > without privilege is reported as unanswered, and the rest of the slice still ships.**  > If any acceptance criterion could pass **vacuously** — an empty diff, an empty set, a skipped gate, a > test that never invokes the function, a grep scoped so narrowly it could not have matched — say so > loudly rather than reporting a pass.  > Report the model and reasoning effort by reading them from your process arguments or provider metadata, > never from a system-prompt identity line. **Write `unknown` rather than a plausible guess.**  > **DO NOT HANG.** Every command runs under a stated timeout — 120s ordinary, 600s build. A command producing no observable progress within its bound is killed and reported. Never run an interactive command. A command you had to kill is a finding worth reporting, not a failure to hide.  > Design calls inside these constraints are yours: decide and report, do not ask. A constraint you find > wrong or impossible is a STOP. **If you stop for any reason**, commit what you have with > `BLOCKED: <reason>` as the first line, push, publish the receipt, and leave the worktree clean. > Report every issue and disagreement, including ones outside this slice's scope. End with the three > UNCLEAR lines — FIRST READ, DURING EXECUTION, REMAINING. "STOP and report" is satisfied ONLY by the > `BLOCKED:` commit path, never by disclosure alone (`PG-EV-03`).  ## Why this exists  The standing close-out rule deploys the live app before every close, and unrated or blocked work stops the deploy instead of shipping silently. SG-233 is rated 98 with nothing open, so its delta ships now: one migration, one model, one writer, three routes, one registration line, one badge.  Waste cut: the SG-233 audit names `slowest_waste = 59s full-suite-raw` — handed over here, not re-derived: candidate suite `730 passed + 2 base-proved environment reds` (pyzbar/tesseract absent, proven on base `7d0cc91`, same family as the standing decoder reds). Full suite stays WAIVED per `PG-DP-02` (restart-gated; substitute below is post-recreate read-only verification of the served tree, and this slice changes zero product bytes itself).  ## G0 — Socket first (lane socket before anything Docker)  Print the lane socket from YOUR unit's own process environment, `export DOCKER_HOST` to it, then prove the server line with `docker version` (the server section must answer — a client-only block is NOT proof). No `docker` invocation of any kind before the export. A default-endpoint call STOPS the slice (`PB-2`).  ## G1 — Pre-state, migrate, one rebuild, one recreate — lane socket only  Establish read-only pre-state: actual ref/hash, clean tree, running image/container ID/Created/RestartCount, health status/body, served JS/CSS/index hashes, alembic head plus authoritative table counts (`PG-EV-08`: the production observable before AND after — counts per table now, counts per table after, plus the new empty `reminder` table at 0 rows).  Then, through the lane socket only and in this order: apply the pending migration (head must become `20261023_sg233_reminder`), build the backend image exactly once, recreate the backend container exactly once (`PG-PR-04`). No host config changes. Second migrate/build/recreate issuance is STOP. `PG-EV-06`: the only rows this slice's run creates are schema plus zero data rows in `reminder`; application row counts must be identical before and after — quote both.  ## G2 — Verify the serve, without claiming more  Observe a new container identity and start time (ID/Created, not RestartCount). Health ok for DB/storage; six health GETs as the standing precedent. Re-read bundle hashes (EXPECTED changed: the Expiry badge ships new JS — record the new hashes, never claim byte-identical), schema head, and table counts; require no slice-caused application row change. Owner-activity deltas get direct discriminating evidence, never hidden.  Suite stays waived per `PG-DP-02`; substitute is post-recreate read-only identity/health/schema/counts/pages verification. Walk the shell routes read-only (`/`, `/expiry` — confirm the Unread reminders section renders — `/inbox`, `/planning`). No writes; no paid calls. Application provider spend prohibited.  ## Constraints  - Scope: `docs/worklogs/SG-234*` only. No product/config/schema/migration/test edits. No production INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, imports or evidence-store mutation. Reads are git / lane-socket docker / curl GETs / read-only SQLite; only executed runtimes are the G0-verified migrate, build and recreate (`PG-IC-01`). Reads/verification ≤120s per command, build ≤600s (inherited precedent); visible timeout finding; idle kill 900s never approached. No prompt or polling loop. No sudo/alternate identity/mount/host config. No secret in any artifact. Runtime-vs-lane budget in seconds from the lane's own stamps (`PG-PR-06`). - Cross-product (`PG-IC-01`): no blanket exclusion is issued. G-hunks share no condition with any remediation step — stops win. Reads MAY use the already-built local images only. - No fixed dates except this header's authoring-date metadata; every time-dependent check reads the live clock at execution (`PG-IC-07`).  ## Acceptance and report  - A1: G0 proven (export + server line) BEFORE any other docker call; one migrate + one rebuild + one recreate THROUGH THE LANE SOCKET; new running-instance proof; health green; explicit deltas (EXPECTED: SG-233 product set served, bundle hashes changed, `reminder` table at 0 rows, all other counts identical); bundle hashes recorded. - A2: base/hash echoed separately; merged-vs-served diff checked against the EXPECTED SG-233 product set; work diff only SG-234 namespace. - A3: command logs + report `docs/worklogs/SG-234_report.md`; elapsed per leg vs lane bound; constraints answered; FIRST/DURING/REMAINING dispositioned. First token SG-234. - A4: notes-ref receipt `refs/notes/storagegenie-coder-reports`, first line `Dispatch-ID: SG-234 | Report: docs/worklogs/SG-234_report.md`, Work-HEAD recorded, push + mapped read-back. Bound lane receipt only.  ## Budget  120s ordinary · 600s build · 1800s overall; REAL metered $0.000000 (zero calls); actual-versus-budget per leg with units.
2400099 opencode run --auto --dir /home/andrei/launcher-desk --model opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash --variant max # LNR-183 — runner: a killed run's tracked work reaches the remote, the OOM victim is named, no restart after a failed verification (`D482`, `D494`)  `PB-1`–`PB-6` bind (`global/CODER.md` §14).  **BASE REF: `main`.** State back the commit it resolved to, as a separate field. **DATABASE: none.** **Restart: none.** **No host install** — engine changes in this repo only; the desk installs later in its own arc (`D495`). This packet is self-sufficient (`D129`).  ## Why this exists  Two defects filed by a project on 2026-10-21 (`#93`, `#94`), design approved by the owner as `D494` after a two-phase second opinion (`docs/reports/LNR-187.md`, `docs/reports/LNR-188.md` — read both; their objections shaped every goal below). Everything below is expected, not certain: line numbers are approximate, check them.  - **`#93` ask 1:** a killed run's WIP snapshot ref `refs/wip/<ID>/<utc>` is local only (`run-coder` ~1658–1680),   so the Architect cannot fetch it. It must not simply be pushed: the snapshot holds untracked, non-ignored files,   and only the basename `.env` is excluded (~1684–1708), so a push could carry a secret off the host. - **`#93` ask 2:** on `Result=oom-kill` (`dispatch` ~1212–1221) nothing names the killed process. Desk probe on   the host, 2026-10-23: the lane user cannot read the kernel log (`dmesg_restrict=1`; `journalctl -k` prints one   "no entries" line); `sudo -n journalctl -k`, which `dispatch` may already use (~1081–1091), shows   `oom-kill:constraint=CONSTRAINT_NONE,…,global_oom,task_memcg=/system.slice/system-dispatch\x2dpip.slice/dispatch-pip@P531.service,task=pytest,pid=417921,uid=1000`.   Bounded to one hour, `grep -F "dispatch-pip@P531.service,"` matched 1 line, 274 B, 0.04 s, 0 other units. - **`#94`:** a Coder whose final check printed `OVERALL FAIL` still wrote `output/dispatch/<ID>.restart_units`,   and `dispatch` (~1242–1323) restarted the services onto that tree. `CO-104` (`global/CODER_PRODUCTION.md`)   already tells the Coder not to; nothing in the runner checks.  ## Goals  **G1 — tracked-only WIP reaches the remote (`run-coder`).** On a kill that produces `wip=<sha> <ref>`, ALSO build a second commit object holding only changes to files tracked at `HEAD` (never untracked files; never any `.env` basename), at `refs/wip-tracked/<ID>/<utc>` (a separate namespace: `refs/wip/<ID>/<utc>` is a leaf ref). Push it **in the same bounded push invocation** as the branch (`GIT_KILL_PUSH_TIMEOUT_S`, ~976–986, ~1960–1970) — no second network call. The partial receipt gains `wip_tracked=<sha> <ref>` | `wip_tracked=none` (no tracked change) | `wip_tracked=failed:<reason>` and `wip_untracked=<n> host_local` (count of untracked files left only in the local snapshot). Existing `wip=` tokens, the local snapshot and the 3 s snapshot bound keep their behaviour; the extra commit object runs inside that same bound or reports `wip_tracked=skipped:time`.  **G2 — the OOM victim is named (`dispatch`).** Only when the unit's `Result` is `oom-kill`: run `timeout 10 sudo -n journalctl -k --since <this run's start> -o cat --no-pager`, keep lines containing the exact string `<this unit's full name>,` (the `task_memcg=` field ends in it), at most 5 lines. From the first `oom-kill:` line take `pid=` and `task=`; `global_oom` present → `oom_scope=global`, else `unit`. Add to the terminal result line, additively: `oom_victim=<pid>/<task> oom_scope=<s>` | `oom_victim=unreadable` (sudo refused or timeout) | `oom_victim=not_found`. **Never print or store any other kernel line.** Take "this run's start" from the same invocation anchor `dispatch` already uses (`_run_inv`, `LNR-180` G2), or say in the report what you used and why.  **G3 — no restart after a failed verification (`dispatch`).** Before applying a marker that G-skip logic did not already skip: read the Coder's report at the same commit the receipt is classified at (reuse `classify_receipt`'s report reader, ~434–533; do not add a second way to find it). Ignore lines whose first non-space character is `>`. If any line starts with `Verification:` (after optional `**`/`-`/spaces), restart only if the LAST such line contains `PASS` and not `FAIL`; otherwise refuse. If there is none, refuse when any remaining line contains `OVERALL FAIL`. A refusal applies no unit and appends `restart=refused_failed_verification` (it ranks with `restart=refused` in the existing order; say where you put it). An unreadable report restarts as today and appends `verification=unread` — visible, never silent. Update `CO-104` in `global/CODER_PRODUCTION.md` with one sentence: end the report with `Verification: PASS` or `Verification: FAIL — <check>` whenever a marker could be written.  **G4 — `tools/gate_plant.py` rows, each proof beside its control.** At least: `wip_tracked_pushed` (kill with a tracked change + an untracked file → origin holds `refs/wip-tracked/<ID>/*` with the tracked change and WITHOUT the untracked file; FAILS on `main`'s engine) · `wip_tracked_env` (a tracked `.env`-basename change never in the pushed object) · `oom_victim_named` (shim `sudo`/`journalctl` on `PATH` returning a fixture with this unit's line AND another unit's line → only this unit's pid/task; FAILS on `main`) · `oom_victim_unreadable` (shim sudo refuses → `oom_victim=unreadable`) · `restart_refused_verification_fail` (`Verification: FAIL` + marker → no restart, token; FAILS on `main`) · `restart_refused_overall_fail` (no `Verification:` line, `OVERALL FAIL` → refused) · guards that PASS on both engines' expectations as stated: `restart_allowed_verification_pass`, `restart_quoted_overall_fail_allowed` (`> OVERALL FAIL` quoted, no verdict line → restart applies). Name every row's control in the report.  ## Order of work — commit early, ONE full suite  1. Read the two review reports and the code ranges above. Write G1–G3. 2. `export GATE_PLANT_LOG=/tmp/lnr183-gate-log.txt` **before every `gate_plant.py` run** — the tracked    `audits/LNR-007r1-gate-plant-log.txt` must not change. Run ONLY the new rows on the candidate → PASS; then the    same rows against copies of `main`'s engines (`GATE_PLANT_RUN_CODER` / `GATE_PLANT_DISPATCH`) → each proof    FAILED_TO_FAIL, each guard PASS. 3. **Commit and publish the receipt NOW**, before any full-suite run — the four commands below, in order. 4. Full `gate_plant.py` **ONCE, on the candidate only. Never a second full run, never a full run on the base**    (`D443`). If an old row fails, re-run that row alone once on the candidate and once on the base and report both;    `killed_hup` is a known flake under load (`ISS-ck`). 5. Update `docs/reports/LNR-183.md` and `docs/reports/LNR-183_verify.log` with the full-suite SUMMARY, then a new    commit and receipt commands 2–4 again on the new `HEAD`.  **Publish before the bound:** read `date -u +%s` at the start and before every step from 2 on; at **2,400 s** elapsed start no new work: commit what is finished, write the report, publish the receipt.  ## Constraints  - **Scope ceiling:** `global/dispatch/run-coder`, `global/dispatch/dispatch`, `tools/gate_plant.py`,   `global/CODER_PRODUCTION.md` (`CO-104` only), `docs/reports/LNR-183.md`, `docs/reports/LNR-183_verify.log`.   Anything else, including `audits/LNR-007r1-gate-plant-log.txt`: stop and report. Before the first commit,   `git status --short` must show only those paths. - **Never use `sudo` yourself; install nothing; write nothing outside the checkout and `/tmp`.** The engine code   you write may call `sudo -n` (G2) — tests use shims, never the real one. - **Hang bound:** each test command under `timeout 900`. - `verify.log` keeps summaries and the new rows' verdicts only, never full suite output. - Every new token is additive: existing tokens keep their spelling and order.  ## Acceptance criteria  G1–G3 as written; G4's rows with the base and candidate verdicts stated; the full suite passes once on the candidate, counts stated; `audits/LNR-007r1-gate-plant-log.txt` byte-identical to `main`; no kernel line other than the matched one appears in any output path.  ## Report and receipt  `docs/reports/LNR-183.md`. First token `LNR-183`; first line: model, effort, cwd, remote, `BASE`, `WORK_HEAD`. `CO-79` start, end, total and per-step elapsed; the `CO-103` list; each goal's code location; each row's control. **This lane binds no `{{RECEIPT_CMD}}`; these are the receipt commands, run in this order:**  1. `git add` your files and `git commit` with the trailer line `Dispatch-ID: LNR-183` 2. `git push origin HEAD:main` 3. `git notes --ref=refs/notes/launcher-coder-reports add -m "Dispatch-ID: LNR-183 | Report: docs/reports/LNR-183.md" HEAD` 4. `git push origin refs/notes/launcher-coder-reports`  A later report fix is a new commit, then steps 2–4 again on the new `HEAD` (use `git notes … add -f` if a note already sits on that commit). Corrections to this packet are invited (`PB-1`).
